# The install's own dependencies, put there by install.sh
.venv/

# Python. **Anchored to the root on purpose.** An unanchored `dist/` matches at every
# depth, and it silently excluded `src/ui/dist` — which is the built console, is committed,
# and is the only thing an installed rundesk has to serve. A `!` negation could not have
# rescued it either: git never descends into an excluded directory, so nothing under one
# can be re-included. The release archive is `git archive`, so untracked is unshipped, and
# the console would have worked on every developer's machine and on nobody else's.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
/build/
/dist/

# The console's workspace: a contributor's tool, never part of an install. Its **build
# output** (`src/ui/dist`) is committed on purpose — that is what lets a user's install
# have no Node, no package manager and no build step. Only the machinery is ignored.
ui/node_modules/
ui/dist/
ui/.vite/
ui/*.tsbuildinfo

# The docs site's machinery. Nothing under `site/` is tracked, and neither line changes
# that — they are here so `git status` stays readable. Anchoring the Python `dist/` above
# stopped it covering `site/dist` by accident, which was the only reason that build was ever
# ignored; and with the build no longer standing in front of it, git descends and lists
# every one of the four thousand files under `node_modules`, which was never ignored at all.
site/node_modules/
site/dist/

# Local environment / credentials — never commit. `.env` is an exact match and does not
# cover `.env.local`, which is where a workspace keeps a developer's own overrides.
.env
ui/.env*.local

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
*.swp

# What a CI step captures a suite into, so a leaked process cannot hold the runner open.
suite.log
suite-logs/
